in Santorini our meekness it can be easy  to get lost  winding roads twisting stairs and narrow  streets can make the city's hard to  navigate on top of that the region is  made up of just two colors white and  blue the bright blue roofs contrasted by  whitewashed walls are an iconic image of  Greece people initially think the colors  originated from the Greek flag are the  bright blue evil eye but actually the  color scheme originated for a different  reason but first it's important to note  that although the colors can be a symbol  for the entire country white walls  paired with bright blue trimmings are a  trademark of the Cyclades which are a  group of Greek islands that include  Santorini Mykonos and Naxos it's also  important to note that Greece wasn't  always white and blue the buildings are  made up of stone mud and volcanic  material which makes them a darker color  locals would use natural ingredients and  lime plaster to add colors to their  buildings for example iron oxide turned  white into a tinted pink if you added  autre you'd get a yellow hue so the  Cyclades ended up being painted on a rad  of earth tones but when Ionis Metaxas  came to power in the 1930s he ordered  all of the buildings to be plastered  with white lime lime was thought to have  antibacterial properties that could  prevent the spread of diseases lime was  also inexpensive and reflected sunlight  that kept the buildings cool during the  summer sea blue originated from a  cleaning agent called la Lakai this was  found and pretty much every home across  Greece a couple tops of this mixed with  lime and you have the bright blue color  we know today when metaxas died the  islands transitioned back to the variety  of natural colors but during the  military dictatorship of 1967 it became  a law for all of the islands buildings  to be blue and white the color showed  patriotism unification and support for  the country's political agenda at the  time  the combination is also historically  known to symbolize white waves crashing  in the Aegean Sea and the blue of the  Greek sky decades later the iconic image  has become ingrained in our minds  although the laws are no longer in place  a majority of the buildings have kept  the traditional color combination so now  if you see the Cyclades from a bird's  eye view you'll notice a blur of bright  white and bold blues dotting each Island  if you visit don't be afraid to get lost  exploring the Cyclades picturesque color  scheme is all part of the fun  [Music]  you
 

